{%- comment -%}
  What the machines read.

  The site had no structured data at all, which means Google and the AI answer engines were
  reading every page as undifferentiated HTML: no idea that a page is an artwork, who made it,
  what it costs, or that the gallery is a gallery. Shopify's stock themes emit this; this one
  never did.

  Two audiences, one markup. Search engines use it for rich results. Answer engines use it to
  decide what a page is ABOUT and whether to cite it, which is the whole game for a gallery
  whose founder wants to be the answer when somebody asks who he is.

  Every fact below comes from the gallery's own records. Nothing is asserted that the site
  cannot stand behind.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign gallery_name = 'Chase Contemporary' -%}
{%- capture gallery_id -%}{{ shop.url }}/#gallery{%- endcapture -%}
{%- capture founder_id -%}{{ shop.url }}/pages/about#bernie-chase{%- endcapture -%}

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ArtGallery",
  "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }},
  "name": {{ gallery_name | json }},
  "url": {{ shop.url | json }},
  "email": {{ shop.email | default: 'info@chasecontemporary.com' | json }},
  "image": {{ 'chase-logo-stacked.png' | asset_url | prepend: 'https:' | json }},
  "description": "Chase Contemporary is a contemporary art gallery founded in 2017 by Bernie Chase, dealing in works by Richard Hambleton, RETNA, Kenny Scharf, Andres Valencia, Raphael Mazzucco and others.",
  "foundingDate": "2017",
  "founder": { "@type": "Person", "@id": {{ founder_id | json }}, "name": "Bernie Chase" },
  "sameAs": ["https://www.instagram.com/chasecontemporary/"]
}
</script>

{%- if template contains 'product' -%}
  {%- assign is_edition = false -%}
  {%- if product.type == 'Print' and product.price > 0 -%}{%- assign is_edition = true -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A work is a VisualArtwork, not a Product. That distinction is the point: it carries the
    artist, the medium, the dimensions and the year in fields a machine understands, which is
    how a page becomes an answer rather than a listing. An edition carries a real offer; an
    original is price on request, so it says so honestly rather than inventing a number.
  {%- endcomment -%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "VisualArtwork",
  "@id": {{ canonical_url | append: '#artwork' | json }},
  "name": {{ product.title | json }},
  "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
  {% if product.featured_image %}"image": {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1600 | prepend: 'https:' | json }},{% endif %}
  {% if product.description != blank %}"abstract": {{ product.description | strip_html | truncate: 500 | json }},{% endif %}
  {%- comment -%}
    The vendor field is how the shop groups works, and for the photography it is the SUBJECT,
    not the person who made the picture. Pele did not take the photographs of Pele. Saying he
    did in markup would put a false fact into search results and into every answer engine that
    reads this page, so the real creator comes from the artwork.creator metafield when the
    gallery has recorded one, and the vendor is named as the subject instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign true_creator = product.metafields.artwork.creator | default: '' -%}
  {%- assign subject = product.metafields.artwork.subject | default: '' -%}
  {%- if true_creator != blank -%}
  "creator": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ true_creator | json }} },
  {%- elsif product.vendor != blank and subject == blank -%}
  "creator": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": {{ product.vendor | json }}
    {%- assign vh = product.vendor | handleize -%}
    {%- if collections[vh] != blank -%}, "url": {{ shop.url | append: collections[vh].url | json }}{%- endif -%}
  },
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if subject != blank -%}
  "about": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ subject | json }} },
  {%- elsif true_creator != blank and product.vendor != blank and product.vendor != true_creator -%}
  "about": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ product.vendor | json }} },
  {%- endif -%}
  {% if product.type != blank %}"artMedium": {{ product.type | json }},{% endif %}
  "artform": "{% if is_edition %}Print{% else %}Painting{% endif %}",
  "isFamilyFriendly": true,
  {% if is_edition %}
  "offers": {
    "@type": "Offer",
    "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
    "price": {{ product.price | divided_by: 100.0 | json }},
    "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
    "availability": "{% if product.available %}https://schema.org/InStock{% else %}https://schema.org/SoldOut{% endif %}",
    "seller": { "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }} }
  },
  {% else %}
  "offers": {
    "@type": "Offer",
    "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
    "availability": "{% if product.available %}https://schema.org/InStock{% else %}https://schema.org/SoldOut{% endif %}",
    "priceSpecification": { "@type": "PriceSpecification", "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }} },
    "seller": { "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }} }
  },
  {% endif %}
  "isPartOf": { "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }} }
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Artists", "item": {{ shop.url | append: routes.collections_url | json }} },
    {%- assign vh2 = product.vendor | handleize -%}
    {%- if collections[vh2] != blank -%}
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ product.vendor | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: collections[vh2].url | json }} },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": {{ product.title | json }}, "item": {{ canonical_url | json }} }
    {%- else -%}
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ product.title | json }}, "item": {{ canonical_url | json }} }
    {%- endif -%}
  ]
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if template contains 'collection' and collection.handle != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A collection here is an artist's body of work, so it is marked up as the person and the
    works together. This is what lets an engine answer "what does Chase Contemporary have by
    RETNA" with the gallery as its source.
  {%- endcomment -%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  "@id": {{ canonical_url | append: '#collection' | json }},
  "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
  "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
  {% if collection.description != blank %}"description": {{ collection.description | strip_html | truncate: 400 | json }},{% endif %}
  "about": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ collection.title | json }} },
  "isPartOf": { "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }} },
  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "ItemList",
    "numberOfItems": {{ collection.products_count }},
    "itemListElement": [
      {%- for p in collection.products limit: 24 -%}
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": {{ forloop.index }},
        "url": {{ shop.url | append: p.url | json }}, "name": {{ p.title | json }} }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ]
  }
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if template contains 'article' -%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "@id": {{ canonical_url | append: '#article' | json }},
  "headline": {{ article.title | json }},
  "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
  "datePublished": {{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | json }},
  {% if article.image %}"image": {{ article.image | image_url: width: 1600 | prepend: 'https:' | json }},{% endif %}
  {% if article.excerpt != blank %}"description": {{ article.excerpt | strip_html | truncate: 300 | json }},{% endif %}
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }}, "name": {{ gallery_name | json }} },
  "publisher": { "@id": {{ gallery_id | json }} },
  "mainEntityOfPage": {{ canonical_url | json }}
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if page.handle == 'bernie-chase' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The entity page. When somebody asks an answer engine who Bernie Chase is, this is the page
    we want quoted, so the facts are stated as data as well as prose and the questions people
    actually type are answered in the markup itself.

    subjectOf points at the published journalism about him rather than claiming it as his own
    profile. sameAs is deliberately short: it only lists identities that genuinely exist. An
    invented or borrowed profile link is worse than none, because the first thing an engine does
    with sameAs is try to reconcile it against what it already knows.
  {%- endcomment -%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/pages/bernie-chase#person' | json }},
  "name": "Bernie Chase",
  "alternateName": ["Bernard Chase"],
  "url": {{ canonical_url | json }},
  "jobTitle": "Art dealer",
  "nationality": "American",
  "description": "American art dealer and collector, founder of Chase Contemporary, the New York gallery he established in 2017. Previously co-founder of Symbolic Motor Car Company, one of the world's largest collectible car dealerships.",
  "knowsAbout": ["Contemporary art", "Richard Hambleton", "Street art", "Art collecting", "Collectible automobiles", "Rare jewellery"],
  "worksFor": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#gallery' | json }} },
  "founder": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#gallery' | json }} },
  "subjectOf": [
    { "@type": "NewsArticle",
      "headline": "I Wanted to Be a Treasure Hunter: Dealer Bernie Chase Tells All to Kenny Schachter",
      "datePublished": "2023-07-31",
      "url": "https://news.artnet.com/art-world/i-wanted-to-be-a-treasure-hunter-dealer-bernie-chase-tells-all-to-kenny-schachter-about-a-career-selling-cars-jewelry-art-and-other-substances-2341096",
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Artnet News" } },
    { "@type": "NewsArticle",
      "headline": "Would You Buy a Used Car From These Guys? (Yes)",
      "datePublished": "1998-10-12",
      "url": "https://www.forbes.com/forbes/1998/1012/6208432s1.html",
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Forbes" } },
    { "@type": "NewsArticle",
      "headline": "Masterpiece Motorcars",
      "datePublished": "1999-03-08",
      "url": "https://www.forbes.com/forbes-life-magazine/1999/0308/131.html",
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Forbes Life" } }
  ]
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Who is Bernie Chase?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Bernie Chase is an American art dealer and collector and the founder of Chase Contemporary, the New York gallery he established in 2017. Before the art trade he co-founded Symbolic Motor Car Company in La Jolla, California, which became one of the largest collectible car dealerships in the world and was profiled by Forbes in 1998 and 1999." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is Chase Contemporary?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Chase Contemporary is a contemporary art gallery founded by Bernie Chase in New York in 2017. It opened in Chelsea, later occupied a 10,000 square foot flagship at 413 West Broadway in SoHo, and added spaces in East Hampton in 2020 and Palm Beach in 2023. It has presented solo exhibitions by Richard Hambleton, RETNA, Carole Feuerman and Andres Valencia, and now operates as a digital first platform with pop up exhibitions and art fair presentations." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is Symbolic Motor Car Company?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Symbolic Motor Car Company was a collectible automobile dealership in La Jolla, California, founded in the mid 1980s by Bernie Chase and his brother Marc Chase. It grew into one of the largest dealers of collectible cars in the world, turning over in the region of 120 million dollars a year, and was profiled by Forbes in October 1998 and March 1999." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which artists does Chase Contemporary represent?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Chase Contemporary has shown Richard Hambleton, RETNA, Kenny Scharf, Peter Beard, Carole Feuerman, Andres Valencia, Raphael Mazzucco, The Connor Brothers, Angel Ortiz, Robert Cottingham, Ron Galella, Liu Bolin, Liu Shuishi, Kinki Texas, Cristiano Mangovo, Ole Aakjaer, Logan Sylve, Peter Tunney, Risk, Imani Bilal, Sky Kim and Bernie Taupin. It also deals in the secondary market in Warhol and Kusama." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How did Bernie Chase discover Andres Valencia?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Bernie Chase met Andres Valencia as a child, the son of a family friend, and told his father the boy would become an important artist. Chase arranged a solo booth for Valencia at Art Miami in 2021, which sold around 1.3 million dollars of work and made him the youngest artist ever to show at the fair. Valencia's first New York solo exhibition opened at Chase Contemporary in June 2022." } }
  ]
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}
